---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import GradientGenerator from '../../components/GradientGenerator.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="CSS Gradient Generator" description="Visually build a linear or radial CSS gradient and copy the ready-to-use CSS.">
  <GradientGenerator client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>Why visual tuning beats guessing color stops</h2>
  <p>Gradients depend on the interplay between color choice, stop positions, and angle — small adjustments can dramatically change the visual result, making a live preview far more efficient than repeatedly editing raw CSS values and reloading to check.</p>

  <h2>How to use this generator</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Choose linear or radial gradient type.</li>
    <li>Pick your colors and adjust the angle or shape.</li>
    <li>Copy the generated CSS gradient value.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Example</h2>
  <p>A diagonal two-color gradient might generate: <code>background: linear-gradient(135deg, #10b981, #6366f1);</code></p>

  <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Using colors with insufficient contrast</strong> for any text overlaid on the gradient, hurting readability.</li>
    <li><strong>Forgetting gradients count as backgrounds</strong>, not actual images — they won't appear in "save image" contexts the way a background-image would.</li>
    <li><strong>Overcomplicating with too many color stops</strong> when two or three well-chosen colors usually produce a cleaner result.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Use cases</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Creating a hero section background</li>
    <li>Building a subtle gradient overlay on a card or button</li>
    <li>Matching a brand's gradient style from a design mockup</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>Can I animate a CSS gradient?</strong> Direct gradient animation is limited in CSS — common workarounds involve animating background-position on an oversized gradient or using multiple layered elements.</p>
</ToolLayout>
